                <h2>为什么要用看板承接开户流程？</h2>
<p>同样的客户资料，在不同代理团队中可能得到完全不同的结果：有人只收到“审核中”，有人不知道缺什么文件，还有人完成开户后才发现支付方式、授权范围或充值币种不匹配。</p>
<p>某工具类 App 曾在三个渠道提交开户资料。由于团队没有统一记录提交版本，客服重复上传了一次营业执照，最终仍需等待人工更正。后来，该团队将每个申请拆成“资料核验、平台提交、审核反馈、账户启用、充值测试”五个状态，才减少了来回沟通。</p>
<p>看板的核心不是展示漂亮数据，而是让每个客户都能明确回答四件事：</p>
<ul>
<li>当前申请进行到哪一步；</li>
<li>卡在哪里，需要补充什么；</li>
<li>由谁负责、何时跟进；</li>
<li>账户启用后还需完成哪些检查。</li>
</ul>
<h2>一、开户前：先建立客户与资料检查表</h2>
<h3>1. 基础信息统一录入</h3>
<p>建议以“客户—平台—申请类型”为三级结构，避免多个客户和多个账户混在一张表中。</p>
<p>每条申请至少记录：公司名称、注册地区、投放地区、目标广告平台、计划开户数量、提交时间、当前状态、负责人和下一步截止时间。</p>
<p>检查项包括：</p>
<ul>
<li>公司主体、官网域名、落地页和付款主体是否一致；</li>
<li>营业执照、经营范围及有效期是否清晰；</li>
<li>投放产品是否属于平台允许的业务类别；</li>
<li>客户是否具备真实的网站、App 或线下经营场景；</li>
<li>联系方式、邮箱及授权人员是否可以正常接收平台通知。</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. 审核资料建立版本记录</h3>
<p>上传营业执照、授权文件或补充说明时，应保留文件名、提交日期和版本号。若平台要求补充材料，旧版本不可直接覆盖，否则无法判断究竟提交了哪一版。</p>
<p>同时设置“待客户确认”“待代理核验”“待平台审核”三种责任状态，避免资料缺失后仍被误标为“处理中”。</p>
<h3>3. 敏感权限单独确认</h3>
<p>开户不等于索取客户的登录密码或安全验证信息。应优先使用平台官方授权、角色分配和双重验证机制，并明确代理人员可查看与可操作的权限范围。</p>
<h2>二、审核中：看板必须显示真实节点</h2>
<p>不要只设置“审核中”和“完成”两个状态。建议至少细分为：</p>
<ol>
<li>资料待补充；</li>
<li>已提交，待平台审核；</li>
<li>平台要求复核；</li>
<li>审核通过，待配置权限；</li>
<li>账户已启用，待测试；</li>
<li>信息不一致或申请被退回。</li>
</ol>
<p>每次状态变化都应写明时间、原因和负责人。对于长期没有反馈的申请，应按照内部 SLA 跟进，而不是反复催促客户重复提交相同资料。</p>
<p>还应记录平台返回的审核备注，但不要把“通过概率”“包开户”等无依据表述写入客户侧看板。专业服务应展示可验证流程和实际节点，而不是承诺无法控制的结果。</p>
<h2>三、启用后：按顺序完成五项测试</h2>
<p>账户显示“已创建”，不代表已经可以正常投放。运营人员可按以下顺序检查：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>权限测试</strong>：客户管理员能否邀请成员、查看账单并撤销授权；</li>
<li><strong>投放测试</strong>：建组、广告系列和素材上传是否正常；</li>
<li><strong>账单测试</strong>：付款方式、币种、税费及额度是否符合计划；</li>
<li><strong>通知测试</strong>：审核、拒审、余额和异常提醒能否送达；</li>
<li><strong>安全测试</strong>：双重验证是否开启，成员权限是否遵循最小化原则。</li>
</ul>
<p>某跨境电商团队曾在账户启用后直接安排大预算上线，随后发现付款方式地区不匹配。增加“五项测试”后，该类问题被纳入开户完成标准，预算上线时间也更可控。</p>
<h2>四、充值与对账：看板不能只显示账户余额</h2>
<p>充值记录建议包含平台、产品、币种、充值金额、汇率、手续费、到账金额、发起人和对账状态。</p>
<p>每日检查待确认、已到账、金额不一致和待退款四类记录；每月再按客户及账户汇总，与平台账单、付款流水和发票记录核对。重点关注小额差异、重复充值及长期未消费余额。</p>
<p>预算预警可按“余额低于安全线”“预计可用天数不足”“单日消耗突增”分别设置提醒，而不是只在余额归零后通知。</p>
<h2>五、异常与风控检查清单</h2>
<p>账户受限、拒审、异常登录或付款失败出现后，先记录影响范围，再决定申诉、补充资料、权限调整或暂停充值。处理期间不要频繁切换设备、代理网络或重复创建相似申请。</p>
<p>每周复盘看板中的四类数据：平均资料准备时长、各审核节点停留时间、一次通过率、账户启用后的异常率。只有把这些指标用于优化资料清单和内部流程，开户服务才真正从“信息转发”升级为可管理的运营体系。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Use a Dashboard for Agency Account Opening?</h2>
<p>Submitting a client’s information is only the first step. Agency account opening involves business verification, platform review, account authorization, billing setup, and exception handling. Without a shared workflow, clients may receive vague updates such as “under review,” teams may upload duplicate documents, and payment or authorization issues may only appear after the account is active.</p>
<p>A practical dashboard should answer four questions: What stage has the application reached? What action or document is required? Who owns the next step, and by when? What checks remain after activation?</p>
<h2>1. Pre-Submission Checklist</h2>
<h3>Create one structured client record</h3>
<p>Use a three-level structure covering client, platform, and application type. Each record should include the company name, registration region, target markets, platform, number of requested accounts, submission date, status, owner, and next deadline.</p>
<p>Verify the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>The company, website domain, landing page, and billing entity are consistent.</li>
<li>The business license and operating details are current and legible.</li>
<li>The product falls within the platform’s permitted categories.</li>
<li>The client has a genuine website, app, or physical business presence.</li>
<li>Contact details and designated account administrators can receive platform notifications.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Track every document version</h3>
<p>Record the file name, upload date, and version number for licenses, authorization documents, and supporting materials. Do not overwrite older files, because teams need to know exactly what was submitted and what the platform requested.</p>
<p>Assign responsibility with clear statuses such as “Awaiting Client Confirmation,” “Ready for Agency Review,” and “Submitted for Platform Review.”</p>
<h3>Confirm sensitive permissions separately</h3>
<p>Account-opening services should not request the client’s password or two-factor authentication codes. Use official platform authorization, role-based access, and two-factor authentication. Clearly define which agency staff can view accounts and which can make changes.</p>
<h2>2. Track the Actual Review Stages</h2>
<p>Avoid a simple “In Review / Completed” workflow. A more useful status model includes:</p>
<ol>
<li>Documentation required;</li>
<li>Submitted for platform review;</li>
<li>Additional verification requested;</li>
<li>Approved, pending configuration;</li>
<li>Active, pending testing;</li>
<li>Returned or information mismatch.</li>
</ol>
<p>Every status change should include a timestamp, reason, and owner. Follow up on stalled applications according to internal service-level targets instead of repeatedly asking clients to resubmit identical documents.</p>
<p>Record platform review notes accurately, but do not present unverified statements such as “guaranteed approval” on the client dashboard. Clients should be able to see the real process and current stage rather than promises the agency cannot control.</p>
<h2>3. Run Five Tests After Activation</h2>
<p>An account being created does not mean it is ready for campaigns. Use this sequence:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access:</strong> Confirm that client administrators can invite users, view billing, and revoke access.</li>
<li><strong>Campaign:</strong> Test campaign setup, ad group creation, and asset uploads.</li>
<li><strong>Billing:</strong> Verify payment method, currency, taxes, and spending limits.</li>
<li><strong>Notifications:</strong> Ensure review, rejection, balance, and security alerts reach the correct recipients.</li>
<li><strong>Security:</strong> Enable two-factor authentication and apply least-privilege access.</li>
</ul>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team once launched a large budget immediately after activation and encountered a regional payment mismatch. Adding these five tests to the activation checklist helped prevent similar issues.</p>
<h2>4. Recharge and Reconciliation</h2>
<p>Every top-up record should include the platform, product, currency, amount, exchange rate, fees, credited amount, requester, and reconciliation status.</p>
<p>Review four categories daily: pending confirmation, credited, amount mismatches, and pending refunds. Reconcile by client and account each month against platform statements, payment records, and invoices. Pay particular attention to small discrepancies, duplicate top-ups, and balances that remain unused for long periods.</p>
<p>Use separate alerts for “balance below the safety threshold,” “insufficient days of available spend,” and “unexpected daily spend growth.” Notifications should not wait until the balance reaches zero.</p>
<h2>5. Exceptions and Risk Controls</h2>
<p>When an account is restricted, rejected, shows unusual login activity, or encounters a payment failure, first document the affected scope. Then choose the appropriate response: submit evidence, correct the application, adjust permissions, or pause top-ups.</p>
<p>Avoid switching devices, networks, or repeatedly creating similar applications while an issue is under investigation.</p>
<p>Review four operational metrics weekly: document preparation time, time spent at each review stage, first-pass approval rate, and post-activation exception rate. These metrics show whether the account-opening service has become a manageable operating system rather than a simple document-submission process.</p>
